WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
ABBY CAREY: Hi.

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.220
I'm Abby, and this
is a quick look

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.280
at how Cloud Code makes getting
started with Secret Manager

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.150
easy.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.790
Hard-coding sensitive
configuration data,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.240
like database passwords
at API keys into strings,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.940
is usually not a good
idea for your code base.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.060
Secret Manager securely
stores this sensitive data

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.820
in Secrets, a project
global object that's

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.850
available to your application
at build or runtime.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:25.740
Cloud Code makes
it easy to replace

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.200
sensitive data in your
code base with Secrets

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.840
that are stored securely
on Google Cloud with Secret

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:31.990
Manager.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.780
You can create and
integrate Secrets

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.690
without leaving your IDE, no
matter what type of application

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:37.650
you're creating.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.610
Cloud Code is available for
JetBrains IDEs, VS code,

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.560
and Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.080
I'm going to run
this demo in PyCharm.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.570
When you run your service
in one of our environments,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.615
we make it easy for
you to add Secrets,

00:00:50.615 --> 00:00:51.990
whether you're
running it locally

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.940
or remotely on Google Cloud.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.880
To start, I've created a
Python Flask, Cloud Run

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.310
service with one of
Cloud Code's samples.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.650
It's a simple hello,
world example.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.640
I've enabled the Secret
Manager API for my GCP project,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.320
added the Secret Manager library
to my requirements.txt file,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.740
and coded up an extra function
that returns the latest version

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.430
value of the Secret.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.160
All the Secret Manager actions
happen in the Secret Manager

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.100
panel.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.790
From here, you can view a
project's existing Secrets

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.950
and create new Secrets.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.470
To create a Secret,
click the Add button

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:31.440
and provide a name and value.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.330
Now that my Secret
has been created,

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:35.910
it shows up in the
Secret Manager panel.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:40.140
To view the value of your Secret
version, go to the version tab,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.350
right-click a version, and
select View Secret Value.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.480
To access this Secret's latest
version value from my service,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.050
I'm going to replace
this message with a call

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:55.170
to the access Secret
version function I added.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.200
In my Secret Manager panel,
I can right-click a Secret

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.440
and get its Secret ID, and then
stick it in the parameters.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.730
Now it's time to
see if this works.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.539
I'll run my service locally
on Cloud Code's Cloud Run

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:10.238
emulator.

00:02:10.238 --> 00:02:12.030
Check the link in the
description with info

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:12.990
on how to do this.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.880
Setup only takes a second.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.400
For local development,
accessing this Secret

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.370
from Cloud Code's Cloud Run
emulator automatically works.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.690
No need to worry
about authentication.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:27.960
And there we have it, the
contents of my Secret.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.240
I can also create a new
version of my Secret

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.325
by right-clicking it and
selecting New Version.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.800
Since the function I added
grabs the latest Secret version

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.560
by default, I can
refresh my service

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.530
and see my new Secret value.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.320
That's a quick look at Cloud
Code's Secret Manager support.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.840
If you have any questions or
feature requests about Cloud

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.640
Code, join our Slack channel.

